\section{\texttt{sshd.c}: the protocol}
\label{src:sshd.c}

An SSH connection goes through these phases, each of them a state of the server ([[stage]]):

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Banner.} Both sides send one line, [[SSH-2.0-]] and the name of the program, ended by CR LF. (They are part of the key exchange hash, without the line end.)
\item \textbf{Key exchange init} ([[KEXINIT]], message 20). Both sides send the lists of the algorithms they know, in the order of their preference; for each
      purpose the first algorithm of the \emph{client's} list that the server also knows is used. We know one of each, so we only check that the client lists them.
\item \textbf{Key exchange} (message 30, 31). The client sends its public key [[Q_C]], we answer with ours ([[Q_S]]), our host key ([[K_S]]) and a signature. Both compute the shared secret $K$. The
      \emph{exchange hash} $H$ is the SHA-256 of everything that has been said so far (both banners, both KEXINIT messages, the host key, both public
      keys and $K$); the signature of $H$ with the host key proves to the client that it talks to the owner of the host key and that nobody changed
      the exchange. $H$ of the first exchange is the \emph{session id}.
\item \textbf{New keys} (message 21). The six keys (the two IVs, two encryption keys and two MAC keys) are cut from SHA-256 of $K$, $H$, a letter A to F and the session id. After
      this message each side encrypts what it sends.
\item \textbf{Services and authentication} (messages 5, 6, 50 to 53): the client asks for the service [[ssh-userauth]] and then tries the method [[none]] (see above).
\item \textbf{Connection} (messages 90 to 100): the client opens a channel of type [[session]], asks for a terminal and a shell, and then
      data flows in both directions in [[CHANNEL_DATA]] messages, controlled by windows: a side may send only as many bytes as the other side has said it has room for.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{A packet} (RFC 4253, section 6) is: a length (4 bytes), the length of the padding (1 byte), the \emph{payload} (the message, its first byte is the number),
the padding (at least 4 bytes, so that the whole packet is a multiple of 16 bytes), and, once the keys are in use, the MAC (32 bytes) behind it. The MAC is the HMAC-SHA-256 of the
sequence number of the packet (4 bytes, counting every packet since the connection began, never reset) and the whole packet \emph{before} encryption. The whole packet,
including the length, is encrypted. To know how many bytes belong to the packet the receiver decrypts the first block (16 bytes) first; the counter mode lets
it decrypt the rest later. The numbers in the packets are big endian ([[get32]], [[put32]] of [[net.h]]); a \emph{string} is a length (4 bytes) and the bytes; an
\emph{mpint} is a number as a string, most significant byte first, without leading zeros and with a zero byte in front when the highest bit is set (so that it is not negative).

